<title>USA: Mass. Senate pledges to cut UPS business.</title>
<headline>Mass. Senate pledges to cut UPS business.</headline>
<dateline>BOSTON 1997-08-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Massachusetts Senate passed a resolution Thursday calling on the senate not to use United Parcel Service until the nation's largest package delivery company resolved its conflict with striking Teamsters.</p>
<p>The resolution, sponsored by Senator Warren Tolman, a former UPS truck driver, orders the Senate to &quot;refrain from services from United Parcel Service until the strike is settled.&quot;</p>
<p>The resolution, which passed on a voice vote, applies only to the Senate itself.</p>
<p>Sen. Tolman, a Democrat from the Boston suburb of Watertown, put on his brown UPS hat when he met with reporters following the vote.</p>
<p>&quot;Fifteen years ago, I wore this hat as a UPS truck driver. Now I'm doing this wearing my hat as a state Senator,&quot; he said.</p>
</text>
